<% @page_title = "How Runwell works" %>
<% client, clients = term(:client).downcase, term(:client, count: 2).downcase %>
<% engagement, engagements = term(:engagement).downcase, term(:engagement, count: 2).downcase %>
<% scope_item, scope_items = term(:scope_item).downcase, term(:scope_item, count: 2).downcase %>
<% work = term(:work).downcase %>
<% types = Setting.current.enabled_labels.map { label_term(it).downcase.then { |name| (name.match?(/\A[aeiou]/) ? "an " : "a ") + name } }.to_sentence(two_words_connector: " or ", last_word_connector: " or ") %>

<%= render "settings/header", current: :help %>

<div class="panel panel--wide shadow center txt-align-start flex flex-column gap">
  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">What it is</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small txt-subtle">
      This is Runwell <%= Runwell::VERSION %>.
      <% if can?(:manage_settings) %>
        <%= link_to "Updates", settings_updates_path, class: "txt-link" %>
      <% end %>
    </p>
    <p>Runwell is the core of the agency’s project management. It answers five questions and nothing else:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Who do we work for, and who can say yes?</strong> <%= clients.upcase_first %> and their contacts.</li>
      <li><strong>What did we agree?</strong> <%= engagements.upcase_first %> and their agreements.</li>
      <li><strong>What is happening against it?</strong> Work, commitments, notes and history.</li>
      <li><strong>What came in?</strong> Requests.</li>
      <li><strong>What needs a person now?</strong> The home page.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">The hierarchy</h2>
    </header>
    <ul class="help-tree">
      <li>
        <strong><%= term(:client) %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle">who we work for</span>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Contacts</strong> <span class="txt-subtle">the people there; some <em>can approve</em> agreements</span></li>
          <li>
            <strong><%= term(:engagement) %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle">what we agreed to do: <%= Setting.current.enabled_labels.map { "a " + label_term(it).downcase + " (" + Setting.current.label_prefix(it) + "-3)" }.to_sentence(two_words_connector: " or ", last_word_connector: " or ") %>, fixed scope or recurring</span>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Agreement versions</strong> <span class="txt-subtle">the initial agreement, then change orders, revisions or add-ons</span>
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <strong><%= term(:scope_item, count: 2) %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle">each line the <%= client %> agrees to, with a price and a staff-only estimate</span>
                    <ul>
                      <li><strong><%= term(:work) %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle">approving adds one <%= work %> item per <%= scope_item %>; add more under it as you go. Each has an owner, a status and a due date</span></li>
                    </ul>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li><strong>Other <%= term(:work).downcase %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle"><%= work.upcase_first %> on the <%= term(:engagement).downcase %> that isn’t one <%= scope_item %>, such as a quick fix or a request made into work</span></li>
              <li><strong>Commitments</strong> <span class="txt-subtle">dated promises, ours or the <%= client %>’s</span></li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><strong>Commitments</strong> <span class="txt-subtle">promises that belong to the <%= client %> rather than one <%= engagement %></span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Requests</strong> <span class="txt-subtle">asks that come in from a <%= client %>, the portal, email or a call, before they are anything else</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="txt-small txt-subtle">Notes, documents and history hang off every level: <%= term(:client, count: 2).downcase %>, <%= term(:engagement, count: 2).downcase %>, <%= term(:scope_item, count: 2).downcase %>, <%= term(:work).downcase %> and requests. Each page links up a level, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> takes you there.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">How work flows</h2>
    </header>
    <ol class="help-steps">
      <li><strong>A request comes in.</strong> A contact sends it from the portal, or you log it from an email or a call.</li>
      <li><strong>You triage it.</strong> It becomes a new <%= engagement %>, a <%= scope_item %> on an open draft, a piece of work or a commitment, or it is dismissed with a reason.</li>
      <li><strong>You draft the agreement.</strong> <%= scope_items.upcase_first %>, prices and the summary the <%= client %> will read.</li>
      <li><strong>You send it.</strong> Sending takes a snapshot, fingerprints it and freezes it. Nothing sent ever changes.</li>
      <li><strong>The <%= client %> decides.</strong> They approve or ask for changes from an emailed link, or you record their decision with evidence such as a forwarded email.</li>
      <li><strong>Approval creates the work.</strong> Every approved <%= scope_item %> becomes <%= work %>, tracked on the <%= term(:work) %> table or board.</li>
      <li><strong>Work moves through its statuses.</strong> Planned, In progress, In review, Blocked, Done. Click the colored circle on any row to change it, or the avatar to reassign. An AI assistant finishes into In review, and a person checks it and marks it Done.</li>
      <li><strong>Changes are new versions.</strong> A change order (fixed scope) or a revision or add-on (recurring) goes through the same send and approve steps.</li>
      <li><strong>You close the <%= engagement %></strong> when it’s done. Its agreement and history stay.</li>
      <li><strong>Your own projects skip the agreement.</strong> Mark the <%= client %> that is you as internal, and its <%= engagements %> are Internal from the start: no price, nothing to send or approve, <%= work %> added straight away.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">How it’s different</h2>
    </header>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No invoicing, no timesheets.</strong> <%= scope_items.upcase_first %> carry the price the <%= client %> agrees to, but nothing is “billable”: everything is <%= types %>. Plugins add time tracking and invoicing (Settings → Plugins) for teams that want them.</li>
      <li><strong>Agreements are frozen once sent.</strong> Nobody can edit what a <%= client %> saw. A change is a new version the <%= client %> approves again.</li>
      <li><strong>State is derived, never typed in.</strong> Each <%= engagement %> is Draft, Sent, Changes requested, Approved, Internal or Closed because of what happened to its versions, not because someone picked it from a list.</li>
      <li><strong>History is append-only.</strong> Every change of consequence is recorded with who did it and where it came from (the app, the portal, an email). Corrections are new entries.</li>
      <li><strong><%= clients.upcase_first %> never have passwords.</strong> They sign in to the portal and approve agreements from signed links that expire.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal estimates stay internal.</strong> <%= clients.upcase_first %> see the frozen snapshot they approved, never estimates or estimate notes.</li>
      <li><strong>@mention a teammate</strong> in any note, description or request by typing <kbd>@</kbd>. They get it in Notifications (bottom left) and by email.</li>
      <li><strong>Home is what needs you.</strong> Questions for you, requests to triage, agreements waiting on a <%= client %>, drafts ready to send, overdue and upcoming commitments, and blocked or overdue work.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Where things are</h2>
    </header>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Runwell</strong> (the logo) is home: what needs a person now.</li>
      <li><strong><%= term(:client, count: 2) %></strong>, then each one’s contacts, <%= term(:engagement, count: 2).downcase %>, commitments, notes and history.</li>
      <li><strong><%= term(:engagement, count: 2) %></strong>, showing active ones by default (approved or internal, and open). Switch the filter to see drafts, sent, changes requested, internal or closed. Each one holds its agreement, scope, work and commitments.</li>
      <li><strong><%= term(:work) %></strong>, as a table or a board, across every <%= term(:engagement).downcase %>.</li>
      <li><strong>Commitments</strong>, open or resolved, ours or the <%= client %>’s.</li>
      <li><strong>Requests</strong>, everything that came in and what it became.</li>
      <li><strong>Search</strong> at the bottom of every page (<kbd>s</kbd>) finds <%= clients %>, contacts, <%= engagements %>, requests, work, commitments and notes. <kbd>/</kbd> in the top bar filters just the page you’re on.</li>
      <li><strong>Add</strong> at the bottom right (<kbd>a</kbd>) is where notes and documents are added, to the record on screen or any other.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">What your <%= clients %> see</h2>
    </header>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Contacts with portal access</strong> sign in from an emailed link, with no password. They see the agreements they were sent, the <%= term(:work).downcase %> and documents you mark visible to the <%= client %>, and can send you requests.</li>
      <li><strong>Contacts who can approve</strong> get the approve or request-changes link when you send an agreement.</li>
      <li><strong>Everything else stays internal</strong>: notes, estimates, history, and any <%= term(:work).downcase %> or document not switched to visible.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Your AI assistant</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small">
      Connect Claude or any MCP app (Settings → Connected apps), or install the command line with
      <code class="txt-x-small">curl -fsSL <%= cli_install_url %> | sh</code> and <code class="txt-x-small">runwell login</code>.
      It works as <strong><%= Current.user.agent&.display_name || "your agent" %></strong>, with your role, and asks before anything reaches a <%= client %>.
      On any piece of work, <kbd>c</kbd> or the ✦ button copies a brief your AI can work from.
    </p>
    <p class="txt-small margin-none">Routines to hand it, word for word:</p>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <li>“Every morning, go through my Runwell briefing and tell me what needs me, most urgent first.”</li>
      <li>“Triage the open requests: for each, suggest what it should become, and ask me before promoting any.”</li>
      <li>“Find overdue work and commitments, and draft a note to each owner.”</li>
      <li>“Before my call with <em><%= client %></em>, summarise everything that happened on their account since last week.”</li>
      <li>“Check my in-progress work against the code and move what’s actually finished to In review, with a note saying how you know.”</li>
      <li>“Read this email and log it as a request for the right <%= client %>.”</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small txt-subtle">Press <kbd>?</kbd> anywhere for this list. Keys work when you’re not typing in a field; a pair like <kbd>g</kbd><kbd>c</kbd> is pressed one after the other.</p>
    <%= render "layouts/shared/shortcut_list" %>
  </section>
</div>
